faqs:
  - q: "How does the Password Generator ensure cryptographic randomness?"
    a: "The generator utilizes the web browser's native window.crypto.getRandomValues API rather than standard Math.random. This provides cryptographically secure entropy generated directly by your operating system's hardware random number pool."
  - q: "Are generated passwords sent or saved on any remote server?"
    a: "No. Password generation occurs 100% locally inside your web browser's memory. No passwords, options, or user data ever leave your device or touch any remote server."
  - q: "What is the recommended password length for strong security?"
    a: "Cybersecurity experts recommend a minimum length of 16 characters combining uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and symbols. Passwords over 16 characters provide exponentially higher entropy resistant to brute-force attacks."
  - q: "Why should I exclude similar characters like 'O' and '0'?"
    a: "Excluding visually ambiguous characters (such as uppercase O vs number 0, or lowercase l vs uppercase I) prevents confusion when typing passwords manually on mobile keyboards or paper backups."
  - q: "Should I reuse generated passwords across multiple accounts?"
    a: "Never. Reusing passwords exposes all your online accounts to credential stuffing attacks if one service suffers a data breach. Always generate a unique password for every account."
